  Explicit Answers to Core Questions

  A. What persistent audit information is genuinely missing today?

  Nothing is missing for Area mutations. Every Area mutation commits an AreaVersion row containing actor (created_by), action (change_type), timestamp (created_at), resulting version, and full area state — all within the same transaction.

  The only marginal gap is the absence of an explicit "action = REGISTERED" record for user registration, but users.created_at implicitly provides this. Login has no persistent trail, which is acceptable for a prototype with email-only auth.

  B. Can AreaVersion satisfy the audit requirement for Area mutations?

  Yes, fully. DESIGN.md explicitly states this. All four audit questions — who, what, when, resulting state — are answered directly by columns on area_version (with a single JOIN to area for workspace context). The existing public API already exposes createdBy on every AreaVersionResponse.

  C. Is a dedicated audit_log table justified? Why or why not?

  No. It would duplicate information already committed atomically in AreaVersion for every Area mutation. It adds one extra write per mutation (50% write amplification for the core operation), produces no new
C. Is a dedicated audit_log table justified? Why or why not?

No. It would duplicate information already committed atomically in AreaVersion for every Area mutation. It adds one extra write per mutation (50% write amplification for the core operation), produces no new information, and contradicts DESIGN.md's explicit intent. The "operations not represented by AreaVersion" clause applies to auth events, which do not warrant a dedicated table for this prototype.

D. Which analytics signals should NOT be persisted and should instead be implemented in Task 16?

All of the following should be Micrometer counters/gauges in Task 16, not DB rows:
- Area mutation counts (area.created, area.updated, area.deleted, area.restored)
- Error event counts (area.version_conflict, area.invalid_geometry)
- Rate limit rejection count (drawing.rate_limit.rejected)
- Auth event counts (auth.register, auth.login)
- WebSocket active connection gauge and event-published counter
- REST latency, error rate, request rate (built-in Actuator/Micrometer)
- HikariCP and JVM metrics (built-in)

None of these belong in PostgreSQL. They are operational signals with no business audit value, they are already adequately captured by application logs at the event level, and their frequency makes DB persistence write-amplification that is not justified.

